* cpu1: losing the supply, and whether the PWM stops before the protection does.
*
* `power_up.cir.in` runs this rail tree upward and asks what arrives when.
* This runs the same tree downward and asks a different question, which
* nothing on this board asked at all: **when the supply goes away, does the
* thing that drives a bridge lose its rail before the thing that stops a
* bridge loses its?**
*
* It is a race and it is not obvious which way it goes.
*
*   - the PWM buffers run from 3V3 and are specified down to 1.65 V;
*   - the over-current comparators run from 5 V and are specified down to
*     2.70 V;
*   - 3V3 comes from a buck whose input *is* the 5 V rail, and that buck
*     holds on until its input falls to its UVLO less its hysteresis - as
*     low as 3.45 - 0.55 = 2.90 V.
*
* So the buck feeding the buffers keeps regulating until 5 V is at 2.90,
* two hundred millivolts above where the comparators stop being specified.
* From there it is a question of how fast each rail decays into its own
* load, which is exactly the sort of thing a closed-form check cannot answer
* and nobody had written down.
*
* **The excitation is the supply vanishing**, not sagging. A connector pulled
* or a fuse opening is the fastest the input can go away, and it is the case
* where the rails have only their own capacitance to live on. A slow sag
* holds 5 V up by regulation for longer and gives the buffers longer to die
* in; it is the gentler direction and it is not what is measured here.
*
* **The load is the declared budget on both rails**, which is the optimistic
* end of the one thing this deck is sensitive to. A lightly loaded board -
* no link on the PHY, nothing drawing from the connector - decays more
* slowly on 3V3 and the margin below shrinks. The board declares no minimum
* load and neither does this deck; what that costs is on the open list
* rather than hidden in a number.
*
* Values come from the build; @path:end@ is an instance path and which end
* of its resolved range to take.

.include ../models/converters.lib

* The input for ten milliseconds, which is twice the longest soft start
* either converter declares and so long enough for everything to be up and
* regulating, and then gone. The step is to zero and not to a lower
* level because what is being measured is the decay, and any residual input
* only feeds the rails.
Vin vin 0 PWL(0 0 100u @input.voltage:nom@ 10m @input.voltage:nom@
+ 10.001m 0 30m 0)

* --- 5 V ---------------------------------------------------------------------
*
* The same divider and converter `power_up.cir.in` uses, with the falling
* thresholds filled in. The LM5164 declares an enable threshold and no
* hysteresis for it, so the model is told to drop out at the same level it
* picks up - which holds 5 V up for the shortest time, and a shorter 5 V is
* the direction that hurts the comparators.
Rlock_top vin lockout @buck5.r_uvlo_top.resistance:max@
Rlock_bot lockout 0 @buck5.r_uvlo_bottom.resistance:min@

Xbuck5 vin lockout n5v buck
+ vout=@rail.5v.voltage:nom@
+ uvlo=@buck5.ic.v_in:min@
+ ven=@buck5.ic.enable_threshold:max@
+ tss=@buck5.ic.soft_start_time:max@
+ ilim=@buck5.ic.load_current_max:min@
+ eff=@buck3v3.efficiency:min@
+ uvlo_off=@buck5.ic.v_in:min@
+ ven_off=@buck5.ic.enable_threshold:min@
C5v n5v 0 {@buck5.c_out1.capacitance:min@ + @buck5.c_out2.capacitance:min@}
R5v n5v 0 {@rail.5v.voltage:nom@ / @rail.5v.current:max@}

* --- 3V3, and the threshold that decides the race ----------------------------
*
* `uvlo_off` is the UVLO at its lowest less the hysteresis at its largest,
* which is the **latest** this converter can stop. Latest is worst here: the
* longer it regulates, the longer the buffers keep a valid supply and the
* closer they come to outliving the comparators.
Xbuck3 n5v n5v n3v3 buck
+ vout=@rail.3v3.voltage:nom@
+ uvlo=@buck3v3.ic.input_uvlo:max@
+ ven=@buck3v3.ic.enable_high_voltage_min:max@
+ tss=@buck3v3.ic.soft_start_time:max@
+ ilim=@buck3v3.ic.load_current_max:min@
+ eff=@buck3v3.efficiency:min@
+ uvlo_off={@buck3v3.ic.input_uvlo:min@ - @buck3v3.ic.input_uvlo_hysteresis:max@}
+ ven_off=@buck3v3.ic.enable_high_voltage_min:min@
C3v3 n3v3 0 {@buck3v3.c_out1.capacitance:min@ + @buck3v3.c_out2.capacitance:min@}
R3v3 n3v3 0 {@rail.3v3.voltage:nom@ / @rail.3v3.current:max@}

.tran 2u 30m

* --- what happened -----------------------------------------------------------

* Where 5 V had got to when the 3V3 converter let go. Between this and the
* comparators' 2.70 V is the whole of the exposure.
.meas tran v_5v_at_3v3_dropout FIND v(n5v) WHEN v(n3v3)=@rail.3v3.voltage:min@ FALL=1

* The two instants the safety property is about.
.meas tran tmp_buffers_lost
+ WHEN v(n3v3)=@safety.buffer1.supply_voltage:min@ FALL=1
.meas tran tmp_comparators_lost
+ WHEN v(n5v)=@trip.fast1_high.supply_voltage:min@ FALL=1

* **And where 3V3 had fallen to at that instant**, which decides whether the
* MCU's own brown-out reset closes the window. The MCU releases
* `PWM_ENABLE_N` to its pull-up when it resets, and that turns the buffers
* off whatever 3V3 is still doing - so if 3V3 is already below the level the
* MCU is guaranteed to have reset at, the exposure is closed by the MCU
* rather than by the rails.
.meas tran v_3v3_at_comparators_lost FIND v(n3v3)
+ WHEN v(n5v)=@trip.fast1_high.supply_voltage:min@ FALL=1

* **The headline.** Positive means the buffers stopped driving while the
* comparators were still specified, which is the only ordering that is safe.
* Negative is a window on every brown-out in which this board emits PWM with
* nothing watching the current.
.meas tran t_protection_margin
+ PARAM='tmp_comparators_lost - tmp_buffers_lost'
